module

-- **Yours (H).** Hand-written, and hand-written *by you*: it arrives as a stub
-- or a `sorry`, and nothing will fill it in. A signature install leaves it
-- alone, so what is put here is kept.
--
-- The central facts about what the calculus *means*, as `sorry` stubs.
--
-- These are what the rule proofs stand on. None of them is about any one rule,
-- and every one of them is about the generated `Spec.lean` and `SmtModel.lean`
-- for *your* operators -- which is why they cannot be inherited and have to be
-- proven per calculus. In Logos this material is `Proofs/Translation*`,
-- `Proofs/TypePreservation*` and `Proofs/Canonical*`: 61,000 lines against a
-- 4,500-line checker layer, and the dominant cost of a new checker.
--
-- They scale with how many SMT theories the calculus takes on, not with how
-- many rules it has. A calculus over Booleans alone owes very little here; one
-- over arithmetic, bit-vectors, strings and sets owes a great deal.
--
-- Order matters: type preservation is what the translation bridge rests on,
-- and both are what the rule proofs use.
--
-- What is *not* here, because it is conditional: if the calculus has
-- binder-sensitive rules it also owes closedness and variable-model results,
-- which belong with the extra invariant in `Proofs/Invariants/Extra.lean`
-- rather than here.
public import @CALCULUS@.Proofs.Invariants.Extra
import all @CALCULUS@.Proofs.Invariants.Extra

@[expose] public section

open Eo
open Smtm

namespace Eo

/-! ## Type preservation

The translation respects typing: an EO term whose translation the semantics
gives a type at all has *the translated type*.

This is the load-bearing one. Without it nothing connects `__eo_typeof`, which
the checker computes, to `__smtx_typeof`, which the semantics computes -- and
the checker's type invariant would say nothing about the model.
-/

/-- The type of a translated term is the translation of its type. -/
theorem eo_to_smt_type_typeof_of_smt_type
    (t : Term) {T : SmtType}
    (_h : __smtx_typeof (__eo_to_smt t) = T)
    (_hT : T ≠ SmtType.None) :
    __eo_to_smt_type (__eo_typeof t) = T := by
  sorry

/-! ## The translation bridge

The equality-oriented form of the above, which is what rule proofs actually
apply: given an EO typing, read off the SMT typing.
-/

/-- Recover the SMT type from an EO typing. -/
theorem eo_to_smt_well_typed_and_typeof_implies_smt_type
    (t T : Term) (s : SmtTerm)
    (hs : __eo_to_smt t = s)
    (hNonNone : __smtx_typeof s ≠ SmtType.None)
    (hTy : __eo_typeof t = T) :
    __smtx_typeof s = __eo_to_smt_type T := by
  subst hs
  have h := eo_to_smt_type_typeof_of_smt_type t (rfl) hNonNone
  exact h.symm.trans (congrArg __eo_to_smt_type hTy)

/--
The Boolean case, specialised.

Used constantly: the checker only ever puts Boolean-typed terms on its stack,
so this is the form most rule proofs need.
-/
theorem eo_to_smt_non_none_and_typeof_bool_implies_smt_bool
    (t : Term) (s : SmtTerm)
    (hs : __eo_to_smt t = s)
    (hNonNone : __smtx_typeof s ≠ SmtType.None)
    (hTy : __eo_typeof t = Term.Bool) :
    __smtx_typeof s = SmtType.Bool :=
  eo_to_smt_well_typed_and_typeof_implies_smt_type t Term.Bool s hs hNonNone hTy

/-! ## Non-vacuity

That a well-formed model exists at all.

Easy to overlook and fatal to omit. Soundness says the assumptions hold in *no*
model; if `model_wf` were satisfied by nothing, that would be true of every
proof and the theorem would say nothing. This is what rules that out.
-/

/-- Well-formed models exist. -/
theorem model_wf_nonvacuous : ∃ M : SmtModel, model_wf M := by
  sorry

/-! ## Canonical values

That evaluating a literal gives a value in canonical form.

The semantics represents values in a normal form -- bit-vectors reduced modulo
their width, maps ordered and without redundant entries -- and rules that reason
about literals need to know the model produces that form rather than merely some
representative of it.

One per literal kind the calculus has. A calculus over Booleans alone owes only
the first.
-/

/-- A Boolean literal evaluates to a canonical value. -/
theorem model_eval_boolean_canonical (M : SmtModel) (b : SmtEval.native_Bool) :
    __smtx_value_canonical (__smtx_model_eval M (SmtTerm.Boolean b)) = true := by
  sorry

end Eo
